    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "FIRST\nFLIGHT\nSan Diego Calif\nPearl HorborT.H.\n7. R.\nLeen\nPATROL SQON 6-F\nU.S. NAvY\n1937\nKank Screwe\nCarried in\n6 - P - 2.\nUNITED STATES POSTAGE\nHE BASE\nSHARD\nJAN\n•ONE FAMI\nUNITED STATES POSTAGE\n2. LEXINGTOI\nPED STATES POSTAGE\nSA FLEC\nJONES\nBONHOMMERICHARD:\nMr. Wm. R. Alley,\n% L. M. Hurtado, CRM\nFleet Air Base,\nPearl Harbor, I.H.",
      "50\n1937, January 28-29\nFIRST\nFLIGHT\nSan Diego Calif\nPearl Horbor T.H.\nPATROL SaDiN 6-F\nU.S. NAvy\n1937\nFlight 50 - Two-color\ncachet used on covers\nNavy Flight, Maneuvers, San Diego-Pearl Harbor\nwelve Navy PBY patrol bombers of Patrol Squadron\n150\nSix took off from the harbor at San Diego at 6:05\na•m., January 28, for Pearl Harbor. They flew the\n2,631 miles in 21 hours and 48 minutes, meaning that\nthey arrived and landed in Hawaii before dawn. These\nlarge seaplanes, under the command of Lt. Cmdr.\nWilliam M. McDade, carried a crew of 80 and flew\napproximately 300 covers. Most of the covers are\ncancelled \"Aircraft Base Force, U.S. Fleet, San\nDiego, Calif., Jan. 28, 1937\" although a few are\ncancelled on January 27. All covers are backstamped\n\"Fleet Air Base, Pearl Harbor, I.H., Jan. 29, 1937\".\nThis marked the first flight to Hawaii from a U.S.\nmainland point other than San Francisco. It also\nmarked the first of many Navy flights between San\nDiego and Pearl Harbor that were to occur before\nWorld War II. On almost all of these Navy flights\nsouvenir covers were flown. (AAMC: TO 1276)"
